35	40	аннотация (OpenAlex)	0	The studied sediments of the Tyumen suite can be characterized by difficulties in confirming oil saturation coefficients derived from well testing data, including the electrical resistivity. Alternative estimation of oil saturation using a capillary model is complicated. This is because the above-mentioned sediments have a complex deposit structure. In some instances, these deposits are limited by a conventionally accepted depth of the lower oil-saturated interval, without justifying the free water level. For this reason, we must estimate deposit heights indirectly using only core analysis data. This article aims to develop indirect methods for evaluating deposit height within the constraints of geological models. To achieve this aim, we first generalized and analyzed capillary properties under both atmospheric and thermobaric conditions. We utilized direct indicators of oil saturation from core fluorescence under ultraviolet light, to determine boundary values for reservoir properties in the zone of maximum saturation. We then selected appropriate values for residual water saturation. Using these values, we compared total porosity with effective and dynamic porosity and obtained consistent boundary values. Based on the established values for residual water saturation and available capillary pressure curves, we calculated capillary pressures and formation heights. As a result, the results show that capillary pressures ranging from 7 atm to 12 atm are sufficient to establish residual water saturation in the Tyumen suite deposits within the studied areas. These pressures correlate to reservoir heights between 90 to 160 m. This approach facilitates the estimation of oil saturation coefficients using the capillary model in reservoirs with uncertain fluid contacts. It can also serve as supplementary tool for justifying individual lenses and blocks within geological models.	2026-10-30 00:20:19.063034+05
36	41	аннотация (OpenAlex)	0	Introduction. The Khanty-Mansi Autonomous Okrug – Yugra (KhMAO-Yugra) is the main oil-producing region of Russia. The impact of oil production factors on the health in the population living in these territories remains an urgent problem.Objective is to compare the rates of prevalence and incidence in the population living in areas with active (group 1) and low oil production (group 2) of KhMAO-Yugra for the period of 2020–2024.Materials and methods. A retrospective study was conducted using data from statistical collections “Health of the population of KhMAO-Yugra”. Morbidity rates (per 100,000 population) were analyzed in children (0–14 years) and adults (18 years and older). The territories were divided into two groups based on official data on oil production volumes.Results. Statistically significant differences were found between the groups. In children from group 1, higher levels of prevalence were recorded for diseases of the blood (p=0.013), circulatory system (p<0.001), and congenital anomalies (p=0.004), as well as higher incidence for congenital anomalies (p=0.022). In adults from group 1, only prevalence of blood diseases was significantly higher (p<0.001). For most other classes (neoplasms, endocrine system diseases, respiratory diseases), higher rates were observed in group 2 (p<0.05). A detailed analysis of the subsoil use structure showed group 2 to include territories with long-term gas production, extraction of solid minerals, and old oil fields, which forms a combined technogenic load.Limitations of the study are associated with the selection of a single region for assessing morbidity rates, restriction to two age cohorts, and the failure to for factors such as population migration activity, socioeconomic status, occupational hazards (work in the oil and gas industry), smoking, and other behavioral risks, as well as the ability to provide medical care in terms of quality and accessibility.Conclusion. Residing in areas of active oil production is associated with increased morbidity rates for classes etiologically linked to ecotoxicants, which requires an in-depth study of risk factors.Compliance with ethical standards. The study does not require the submission of a biomedical ethics committee report or other relevant documents.Contributions: Ibragimova S.Sh. – research concept and design, data collection and processing, text writing, editing;Ushakova O.V. – research concept and design, text writing, editing;Evseeva I.S. – data collection and processing, text writing, editing. All authors are responsible for the integrity of all parts of the manuscript and approval of the manuscript final version.Funding. The study was performed within the framework of the state assignment and dissertations.Conflict of interest. The authors declare no conflict of interest.Received: April 2, 2026 / Accepted: May 20, 2026 / Published: June 18, 2026	2026-10-30 00:20:19.063034+05
37	42	аннотация (OpenAlex)	0	This paper presents the results of a comprehensive study of soils in the limnic landscapes of the subtaiga within the Tura-Tavda interfluve (southwestern part of West Siberia). A significant portion of research on the soils of lake-bog landscapes in the subtaiga of the southern Tyumen Oblast has focused on the properties and ecosystem functions of peatlands within large bog massifs, as well as their transformation during reclamation efforts and the subsequent utilisation of drained lands. However, less attention has been devoted to the hydromorphic and semi-hydromorphic soils of lake basins. Accordingly, the aim of this study is to investigate the diversity, morphological characteristics, and prevailing processes in the soils of lake basins in the subtaiga of the Tura-Tavda interfluve, using Lake Kuchak and several adjacent water bodies within the Tyumen carbon test site as examples. The study area is situated in the southeastern part of the Tura-Tavda interfluve, a gently undulating, flattened depression plain with elevations ranging from 80 to 105 metres. In the peripheral areas, this surface transitions into a terraced plain characterised by lake-marsh and swampy flat-depression features, with absolute elevations of 60 to 70 metres. Fieldwork was conducted in the summer of 2025, encom-passsing the area surrounding the Tyumen State University biological station on Lake Kuchak (the Tyumen Carboniferous Test Site) and the Guzeneyevo Nature Reserve. Soil-morphological and soil-geomorphological methods were employed. Specific 7 science fieldwork locations were selected based on reconnaissance route data, as well as analysis of satellite imagery and topographic maps. A total of nine full-profile transects were established and studied, representing various parts of the basins of lakes Bolshoe Miyagi, Kuchak, Tangachi, Chertankul, and Yantykovo, as well as transitional and watershed surfaces (See Fig. 1). The study methods included a detailed description of the morphological features of pedons grouped according to different geomorphological levels of lake basins. Analytical methods involved determining the main soil properties, including the measurement of pH in pHhO and pHKcl suspensions using the potentiometric method; organic carbon (Cojg) content using the Tyurin dichromate method; exchangeable Ca2+ and Mg2+ contents using the complexometric method; oxalate-soluble iron compounds (Feo) in Tamm extracts and non-silicate iron compounds (Fed) in Mehlich-3 extracts, both quantified spectrophotometrically; and carbonate content using the acidometric method. Soil horizon colour was determined using a spectrophotometer, with data presented in the Munsell and CIE Lab colour systems. Particle size distribution was analysed using a laser diffraction particle size analyser following pre-treatment of soil samples with sodium pyrophosphate. Micromorphological studies were conducted on transparent thin sections using a polarising microscope. Based on the research findings, the principal patterns of landscape-geomorphological organisation of the soil cover in the study area were identified. Soils in the study area can be categorised into several groups according to their position within the relief, morphological characteristics, and physicochemical properties (See Figs. 2 and 3). It was determined that humus-gley soils (Folic Histosols) predominate in the lake floodplain, developing on relatively poorly sorted lacustrine-alluvial sediments enriched with biogenic silica. This soil group is characterised by predominant organic matter accumulation, gleyzation, and carbonate formation. The soil profile exhibits significant vertical heterogeneity due to lenses and interlayers of mineral material, as well as horizons of buried peat soils (See Fig. 4). The formation of CaCOg-enriched zones is constrained by a specific combination of conditions. Micromorphological studies enabled us to hypothesise the complex spatiotemporal dynamics of this process and its close relationship with the water chemistry and hydrological regime of the studied reservoirs. Soils on gentle slopes and limnic terraces (Gleyic Arenosols) demonstrate a gradual transition to autonomous soil formation (See Fig. 5). They are characterised by a two-layered depositional pattern, with thick aeolian sediments overlying mineralised lacustrine-alluvial deposits, upon which the eluvial horizon develops. These soils exhibit low contrast in the upper profile and relatively weak humus accumulation processes. A key outcome of the study was the confirmation of the hypothesis regarding the potential significance of lake basin soils for assessing carbon reserves and key biogeochemical processes in the subtaiga zone of the southwestern part of West Siberia. The article contains 5 Figures, 57References, and 1 Supplement. The Authors declare no conflict of interest.	2026-10-30 00:20:19.063034+05
