550	553	аннотация (OpenAlex)	0	A Novel Approach to Fracturing Height Control Enlarges the Candidate Pool in the Ryabchik Formation of West Siberia's Mature Oilfields Kreso Kurt Butula; Kreso Kurt Butula Schlumberger Search for other works by this author on: This Site Google Scholar Alexey V. Yudin; Alexey V. Yudin Schlumberger Search for other works by this author on: This Site Google Scholar Yuri V. Novikov Yuri V. Novikov OAO TomskNeft VNK YUKOS EP Search for other works by this author on: This Site Google Scholar Paper presented at the European Formation Damage Conference, Scheveningen, The Netherlands, May 2007. Paper Number: SPE-107604-MS https://doi.org/10.2118/107604-MS Published: May 30 2007 Cite View This Citation Add to Citation Manager Share Icon Share Twitter LinkedIn Get Permissions Search Site Citation Butula, Kreso Kurt, Yudin, Alexey V., and Yuri V. Novikov. "A Novel Approach to Fracturing Height Control Enlarges the Candidate Pool in the Ryabchik Formation of West Siberia's Mature Oilfields." Paper presented at the European Formation Damage Conference, Scheveningen, The Netherlands, May 2007. doi: https://doi.org/10.2118/107604-MS Download citation file: Ris (Zotero) Reference Manager EasyBib Bookends Mendeley Papers EndNote RefWorks BibTex Search nav search search input Search input auto suggest search filter All ContentAll ProceedingsSociety of Petroleum Engineers (SPE)SPE European Formation Damage Conference and Exhibition Search Advanced Search AbstractThe productive pay of the low permeability Ryabchyk formation in the mature fields of Western Siberia is separated from underlying water zones by a weak stress barrier.Operating and service companies alike applied various techniques to prevent the breakthrough of hydraulic fractures into the underlying water zone, but so far without clear success.The paper describes a technique of physical barrier placement and tailoring fracturing fluid systems to control fracture net pressure development, that combined is used to mitigate fracture height growth. The method consists of pumping a predetermined mixture of specialty solid materials. The case study clearly demonstrates the challenges encountered in the attempt to increase the fracture half-length in order to improve the fracture treatment and the increasingly difficult task of simultaneously controlling fracture height growth. In addition to the risk of the post-frac increase in water-cut, the uncontrolled fracture height growth into the water zone is also considered to be a major cause of premature screen-outs. This is particularly true in cases when large mesh-size intermediate strength proppants are used.The systematic application of the technique resulted in the increase of the average measured net pressure from 14 to 27 bars at the end of the main treatment, indicating fracture containment within the pay zone. The contained fractures manifest a 4 times liquid and 3.2 times higher average oil production compared to the production before the fracturing treatment. The new fracturing method, when compared to the conventional fractures placed in the past, resulted in the decrease the post-frac water cut from 75 to 46% while at almost no impact in average oil rate.The paper provides a summary of the solution, which allowed the operating company to proceed with successful fracturing activity in the Ryabchyk formation.IntroductionFailure to contain fracture height growth during hydraulic fracturing treatments often renders uneconomical results which drastically alter pay-out time, overall hydrocarbon recovery and profitability. Problematic production of water from outside the zone of interest can rarely be reversed. Here the operator incurs the additional expense of water disposal and decreased hydrocarbon inflow at the wellbore. Likewise, vertical growth into a gas cap is equally undesirable from the standpoint of decreased primary recovery.In Western Siberia, where highly conductive Tip Screen Out 1 (TSO) treatments and large mesh size (12/18 mesh and larger) Intermediate Strength Proppants (ISP) are regularly pumped, fracture height growth is additionally considered to be a major cause of premature screen outs. The fracture height growth out of the pay zone and the reduced proppant mass placed because of the premature treatment termination will ultimately also have an impact on the frac productivity.For that purpose many operating and service companies in Western Siberia have applied various techniques to prevent the breakthrough of the propped fracture, but so far without a clear and documented success.This paper describes a combined technique to control fracture height growth through the use of selective placement of artificial barriers to proppant transport that are placed adjacent to the pay zone, adjusted frac fluid systems and pump schedules. These purposely -created barriers are placed prior to the main fracturing treatment by pumping a mixture of specialty particles.The method is not unique to Western Siberian conditions and may be applied to any formation where height growth is suspected to occur at the expense of fracture extension.Fracture height growth controlFracture height is mainly controlled by the in-situ stresses, in particular by differences in the magnitude of stress between various lithological layers. More formally, fracture height is controlled by the ratio of net pressure to stress differences's. Several methods of restricting fracture height during hydraulic fracturing treatments have been tried where lithological barriers are known to be weak or nonexistent. Keywords: fracturing fluid, proppant, oilfield, hydraulic fracturing, containment, artificial barrier, western siberia, fracturing materials, Upstream Oil & Gas, oil production Subjects: Hydraulic Fracturing, Fracturing materials (fluids, proppant) This content is only available via PDF. 2007. Society of Petroleum Engineers You can access this article if you purchase or spend a download.	2026-10-30 00:20:19.063034+05
551	553	аннотация (OpenAlex)	0	Abstract The productive pay of the low permeability Ryabchyk formation in the mature fields of Western Siberia is separated from underlying water zones by a weak stress barrier. Operating and service companies alike applied various techniques to prevent the breakthrough of hydraulic fractures into the underlying water zone, but so far without clear success. The paper describes a technique of physical barrier placement and tailoring fracturing fluid systems to control fracture net pressure development, that combined is used to mitigate fracture height growth. The method consists of pumping a predetermined mixture of specialty solid materials. The case study clearly demonstrates the challenges encountered in the attempt to increase the fracture half-length in order to improve the fracture treatment and the increasingly difficult task of simultaneously controlling fracture height growth. In addition to the risk of the post-frac increase in water-cut, the uncontrolled fracture height growth into the water zone is also considered to be a major cause of premature screen-outs. This is particularly true in cases when large-mesh-size intermediate strength proppants are used. The systematic application of the technique resulted in the increase of the average measured net pressure from 14 to 27 bars at the end of the main treatment, indicating fracture containment within the pay zone. The contained fractures manifest a 4 times liquid and 3.2 times higher average oil production compared to the production before the fracturing treatment. The new fracturing method, when compared to the conventional fractures placed in the past, resulted in the decrease the post-frac water cut from 75 to 46% while at almost no impact in average oil rate. The paper provides a summary of the solution, which allowed the operating company to proceed with successful fracturing activity in the Ryabchyk formation.	2026-10-30 00:20:19.063034+05
