616	613	аннотация (OpenAlex)	0	The condensate pools of some of the multi-pay fields of We st Siberia (Urengoy, Zapolyar, Novo-Portov, and others) are characterized by successive change in composition and physical-chemical properties of the condensates upward along the section only within individual members or formations. The concentra tion of alkanes increases, as do es the number of branching compounds among the methane hydrocarbons. Concentrations of cyclanes and arenes ha ve an opposite distribution. At contacts between litho-stratigraphic units this pattern of distribution is disrupted. Consequently, the changes in composition are due t o differentiation within a unit and not to vertical migrational separation. Hydrocarbons did not migrate from lower horizons vertically into upper. Comparison of the physical-chemical properties of oils of two-phase and one-phase pools in specific regions shows that the oils of the oil rings are typical of the oils of the oil-gas region. However, the curves of the group composition of the hydrocarbon s of corresponding intervals of boiling of oils of rings and condensates of gas caps for several gas-condensate-oil pool s of West Siberia are not similar; they show anomalous relationships of individual groups of hydrocarbons. In some cases almost in al l fractions the content of aromatic hydrocarbons is higher in the condensate than in the oil, and in others the condensate contains less methan e hydrocarbons than does the oil. This indicates the absence of equilibrium between the compositions of the liquid and gas phases and suggests the low probability of formation of oil fringes from precipitation of liquid hydrocarbons from retrograde solution . The condensate pools of West Siberia formed after the oil pools had formed. This is indicated by the abundant residual oil saturation in the gas caps of many two-phase pools and gas-condensate pools proper. In the northern region s of West Siberia the accumulations of liquid hydrocarbons in the Jurassic and Neocomian sediments were formed before the inversion of the tectonic regime that took place during the Neogene. The Neogene of West Siberia was a time of uneven uplift and erosion and consequent drop in formati on pressure in the oil-gas complexes by 1.5-4.0 mpa. The amount of gas released from the oil by this process wo uld not exceed 3-4% of the actual amount of gas of the gas caps. This drop in formation apparently released gas dissolved in the formation waters in amounts sufficient to form the main reserves of the gas fields of the region. The formation waters of the Mesozoic sediments of West Siberia are regionally saturated with hydrocarbon gases amount released by a 2 mpa drop in formation pressure has been estimated at 440-500 trillion m 3 . After the oil pools had formed in the traps, the later released gas expelled the oil, leaving only residual oil in the gas caps. Since the pressure of beginning of condensation in most condensate pools of West Siberia is close to the formation pressure, content of condensate can be predicted on the basis of only te mperature and pressure. Geothermal gradient becomes a factor here. In the Jurassic complex the predicted condensate content has a range from 100 to 400 g/m 3 and more. See Fig. 1. This map can be used in planning exploration. © Petroleum Geology: A digest of Russian literature on Petroleum Geology, 2013	2026-10-30 00:20:19.063034+05
